--TEST--
`Core\Http\Response::tls` answers `?Core\Http\TlsInfo`, so every member of it is reachable behind a null check and none of them off the answer itself — `rule:http-server/a-reply-reports-its-tls-session`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: the read the language accepts
// and the read it refuses, named together. A `tls()` that answered
// `Core\Http\TlsInfo` rather than its nullable form would pass the second half
// of this and hand every program that never calls over TLS a value describing a
// session that does not exist.
Core\Http\Response $r = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/thing");

// Accepted: the check is what a reply with no session is told apart by, and
// past it all seven members are ordinary reads.
var $info = $r->tls();
if ($info != null) {
    echo $info->version(), " ", $info->cipher(), "\n";
    echo $info->verified() ? "verified" : "relaxed", "\n";
    echo Core\Arr::count($info->peerChain()), "\n";
    echo $info->subject(), " ", $info->issuer(), "\n";
    echo $info->expiry()->toIso(), "\n";
}

// Refused: a plain `http` reply and one a test's table answered arrived over no
// session at all, so the answer is `null` often enough that reading straight
// through it is a program that works until the first call that did not
// negotiate anything.
echo $r->tls()->version(), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0459]: `null|Core\Http\TlsInfo` may be `null`, so `->` cannot reach a member of it
   --> case.nvs:24:6
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
